RBAC
│
├── Role
├── RoleBinding
├── ClusterRole
└── ClusterRoleBinding



serviceaccount.yaml    Creates an identity for a Pod/application to use when accessing Kubernetes resources

Easy example
Suppose your Pod needs permission to read Pods.

ServiceAccount
      ↓
RoleBinding
      ↓
Role
      ↓
Permission: read Pods
      ↓
Pod uses ServiceAccount

So:
User → can use RBAC permissions
Pod/Application → usually uses a ServiceAccount
Role/ClusterRole → defines what they can do
RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding → connects the identity to those permissions


Imagine you have an application running inside a Pod:
Pod
 └── Application

The application needs to ask Kubernetes:   "Show me the list of Pods."
Kubernetes needs to know:     Who is asking?

The Pod needs an identity. That identity is a ServiceAccount.

Pod
 ↓
ServiceAccount = my-app
 ↓
RBAC permissions
 ↓
Can read Pods


Real example
Suppose you have a monitoring application:
Monitoring Pod
      ↓
needs to read Pod information
      ↓
SrviceAccount
      ↓
Role
      ↓
"Can read Pods"

Without the proper ServiceAccount/RBAC permission:
Monitoring Pod
      ↓
"Give me Pods"
      ↓
❌ Permission denied

With it:
Monitoring Pod
      ↓
ServiceAccount: monitoring
      ↓
Role: read Pods
      ↓
✅ Kubernetes allows it

A ServiceAccount is NOT a person/user.
Identity	Used by
User	Human, like developer/admin
ServiceAccount	Pod/application



Step 1 — ServiceAccount

You already have:
rbac/
├── serviceaccount.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: dev

Apply it:  kubectl apply -f serviceaccount.yaml


Step 2 — Create Role
Now decide what the ServiceAccount is allowed to do.
For example: allow my-app to get and list Pods.

role.yaml
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: dev

rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]

Apply:   kubectl apply -f role.yaml


pod-reader
   ↓
Can get Pods
Can list Pods


Step 3 — Connect ServiceAccount to Role
The Role exists, but my-app doesn't have it yet.

Create rolebinding.yaml:
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: my-app-binding
  namespace: dev

subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: my-app
  namespace: dev

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Apply:    kubectl apply -f rolebinding.yaml

Now:
ServiceAccount
    my-app
       ↓
 RoleBinding
       ↓
    Role
 pod-reader
       ↓
 get/list Pods





Step 4 — Make the Pod use the ServiceAccount
In your Pod/Deployment:
spec:
  serviceAccountName: my-app

For example:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: dev

spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app

    spec:
      serviceAccountName: my-app

      containers:
      - name: app
        image: nginx



No. serviceAccountName: my-app cannot be given anywhere. It has a specific location.
It must be under the Pod's spec.


Why here?
Because you are telling Kubernetes:
"Pods created by this Deployment should use the my-app ServiceAccount."

The flow is:
Deployment
   ↓
creates Pod
   ↓
Pod uses ServiceAccount: my-app
   ↓
RoleBinding
   ↓
Role
   ↓
Permissions

You don't put serviceAccountName inside the Role or RoleBinding.
The ServiceAccount is connected to the Pod here:

spec:
  serviceAccountName: my-app

And the RoleBinding connects that ServiceAccount to the Role:
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: my-app
  namespace: dev

So there are two different connections:
Pod ←── serviceAccountName ──→ ServiceAccount
ServiceAccount ←── RoleBinding ──→ Role




Now the complete flow is:

              Kubernetes
                  │
            ServiceAccount
              "my-app"
                  │
                  ↓
            RoleBinding
                  │
                  ↓
               Role
           "pod-reader"
                  │
                  ↓
        get/list Pods permission
                  │
                  ↓
                 Pod
What about ClusterRole?

After understanding this, the next concept is:
Role              → permissions within ONE namespace
ClusterRole       → permissions across the cluster

Then:  Role + RoleBinding
and
ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding

So your RBAC folder can be:
rbac/
├── serviceaccount.yaml
├── role.yaml
├── rolebinding.yaml
├── clusterrole.yaml
└── clusterrolebinding.yaml

For now, focus on these 3: ServiceAccount → Role → RoleBinding. Once this is clear, ClusterRole becomes much easier.


If your current ServiceAccount is not in dev
without namespace, it was created in the default namespace.
You cannot move that ServiceAccount from default to dev. Create it again in dev:


Resource	           Scope
Role                	One namespace
RoleBinding         	One namespace
ClusterRole              	Cluster-wide / can define permissions for cluster-scoped or namespaced resources
ClusterRoleBinding   	Cluster-wide

So remember:  Role = permissions for one namespace.




Role
Role = permissions within one namespace.
Cluster
│
├── dev
│   └── Role → permissions in dev
│
└── prod
    └── Role → permissions in prod

For example:
kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: dev

It can give permission to resources such as Pods, Services, ConfigMaps, etc. in dev only.



ClusterRole
ClusterRole = permissions that are not limited to one namespace.

It can be used for:
Resources across multiple/all namespaces
Cluster-scoped resources such as Nodes, PersistentVolumes, Namespaces, etc.



Example:
ClusterRole
     ↓
Can view Pods
     ↓
dev Pods
prod Pods
test Pods

So your understanding can be remembered as:
Role → one namespace
ClusterRole → cluster-level / can be used across namespaces

⚠️ One important point: ClusterRole doesn't automatically mean "access to everything in the cluster." Its actual permissions depend on the rules: you define.

-----------------------
ClusterRole
ClusterRole defines permissions that are not restricted to one namespace.

For example, suppose you want an application to read Pods in all namespaces:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole

metadata:
  name: pod-reader

rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]

Notice there is no namespace: here.


Important
A ClusterRole by itself does not give anyone permission.
You still need a binding:
ClusterRole
     ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
     ↓
ServiceAccount / User

For example:

ServiceAccount: my-app
          ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
          ↓
ClusterRole: pod-reader
          ↓
Can read Pods across namespaces


So remember:

Role = permissions for one namespace
ClusterRole = reusable permissions at cluster level
Binding = gives those permissions to an identity.



you're asking how ClusterRole can give permission to Nodes.
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]


-----------------------------------

This tells Kubernetes which API group the resource belongs to.

"" means the core API group.

For example, these are core API resources:

Pods
Services
Nodes
Namespaces
ConfigMaps
Secrets

So for nodes, we use:   apiGroups: [""]




2. resources: ["nodes"]
resources: ["nodes"]

This tells Kubernetes:  Which resource are we giving permission for?
Here:  nodes
means the ServiceAccount can access Kubernetes Node objects.



3. verbs: ["get", "list"]
verbs: ["get", "list"]

This tells Kubernetes:  What can they do with Nodes?
get = view one specific Node
kubectl get node node-1
list = view all Nodes
kubectl get nodes




1. apiGroups

This tells Kubernetes which API groups the resources belong to.

For example:

""                       → core resources
apps                     → Deployments, ReplicaSets, DaemonSets
autoscaling              → HPA
batch                    → Jobs
policy                   → PodDisruptionBudget, etc.
rbac.authorization...    → RBAC resources

So you are saying:

"I want permissions for resources from these API groups."

2. resources

This tells Kubernetes which resources.

For example:

pods
deployments
services
nodes
secrets
configmaps
persistentvolumes
persistentvolumeclaims

So this is saying:

"I want to give permissions on these resources."

3. verbs

This tells Kubernetes what actions are allowed.

verbs:
- get
- list
- watch
- create
- update
- patch
- delete









apiGroups means which Kubernetes API group the resource comes from.

You can think of it like categories/folders of Kubernetes resources.

For example:

Kubernetes API
│
├── Core API group ("")
│   ├── Pods
│   ├── Services
│   ├── Secrets
│   └── Nodes
│
├── apps
│   ├── Deployments
│   ├── ReplicaSets
│   └── DaemonSets
│
├── batch
│   ├── Jobs
│   └── CronJobs
│
└── autoscaling
    └── HorizontalPodAutoscaler

So when you write:

apiGroups: [""]
resources:
- pods

it means:

Pods from the core API group.

When you write:

apiGroups: ["apps"]
resources:
- deployments

it means:

Deployments from the apps API group.

Why do we need it?

Because Kubernetes has resources with different API groups. Kubernetes needs to know exactly which resource you're giving permission to.

So remember:

apiGroups → Which category?
resources → Which resource?
verbs     → What can they do?

---------------------------------------------------


Role and ClusterRole are not specifically for users or applications. They define what permissions are allowed.
They can be assigned to either users or applications.



To attach a Role/ClusterRole to a user or application, you use a Binding.

1. Attach Role to a user

First create the Role:

kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: dev

rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list"]

Then attach it to the user using RoleBinding:

kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-binding
  namespace: dev

subjects:
  - kind: User
    name: john

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Flow:

User: john
     ↓
RoleBinding
     ↓
Role: pod-reader
     ↓
get/list Pods in dev
2. Attach Role to an application

Create a ServiceAccount:

kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: my-app-sa
  namespace: dev

Then RoleBinding:

kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: app-binding
  namespace: dev

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: my-app-sa
    namespace: dev

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io


Then tell the Pod/Deployment to use that ServiceAccount:
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: my-app-sa

Flow:
Deployment
    ↓
   Pod
    ↓
ServiceAccount
    ↓
RoleBinding
    ↓
Role
    ↓
Permissions




3. What about ClusterRole?

Same idea, but the binding can use a ClusterRole:

User / ServiceAccount
        ↓
RoleBinding
        ↓
ClusterRole

or:

User / ServiceAccount
        ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
        ↓
ClusterRole

The important difference is RoleBinding vs ClusterRoleBinding controls the scope of the assignment.

Easy memory:

Role = permission
Binding = attach permission
ServiceAccount = application identity













